---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import { stringTuple } from "../../lib/source.ts";

const M = "server/src/iki_server/runs/models.py";
const P = "server/src/iki_server/runs/provisioner.py";
const C = "spec/src/iki_spec/compute.py";
const tiers = stringTuple(M, "TIERS");

const H = {
  short: "Where a run's machine comes from",
  how: "The tiers and the provisioner",
  example: "The dev cluster's compute",
  rules: "Templates, warm pods, Slurm and machines",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      An action says which tier it runs on and in which environment. Every tier runs the same
      worker binary; what differs is who starts the machine and how long it lives. The tiers in the
      code are <code>{tiers.join(", ")}</code> (<Path p={M} sym="TIERS" />), plus a <code>server</code>{" "}
      tier for core actions Iki's task runner carries out itself (<Adr n="0178" />). Plugins declare
      environments; each deployment's reviewed <code>compute.yaml</code> declares the machines
      (templates) and which environments run on which (<Adr n={[9, 41]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Machines come from</th><th>Code</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>backend</code></td><td>The chart's worker Deployment: baked, signed images, one credential per pod.</td><td><Path p="deploy/helm/iki/templates/worker-deployment.yaml" /></td><td><Adr n="0048" /></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>container</code></td><td>The provisioner: a Kubernetes Job per run (<code>k8s_job</code>), or warm pods that take one job after another (<code>k8s_deployment</code>).</td><td><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/kubernetes.py" sym="def job_manifest(" /></td><td><Adr n={[61, 62]} /></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>container</code> on Slurm</td><td>A worker on a cluster's login node submits each job with <code>sbatch</code> where the server placed it.</td><td><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/slurm.py" sym="def place(" /></td><td><Adr n={[133, 156, 167]} /></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>vm</code></td><td>The provisioner: an EC2 or Compute Engine instance per run, running <code>iki-worker host</code>; volumes from snapshots.</td><td><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/ec2_fleet.py" sym="class Ec2Fleet" />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/gce_fleet.py" sym="class GceFleet" /></td><td><Adr n={[74, 76, 109, 160]} /></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>self_hosted</code></td><td>A team's own machines, registered by a Compute admin; they lease only their teams' projects' runs, by labels.</td><td><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/workers.py" sym="def covered(" /></td><td><Adr n="0112" /></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>server</code></td><td>Iki's own task runner, for core actions such as a spreadsheet import.</td><td><Path p={M} sym="SERVER_TIER" /></td><td><Adr n="0178" /></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>The provisioner</h3>
    <p>
      <code>iki-server provision</code> is the only part of Iki that may talk to the Kubernetes API
      or start cloud machines. One acts at a time, under a Postgres advisory lock, a round every few
      seconds and as soon as a job is queued on its tiers (<Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/runs/management/commands/provision.py"
      />, <Path p="deploy/helm/iki/templates/provisioner.yaml" />). Each <code>tick</code>, for each
      fleet (Kubernetes pods, EC2 or Compute Engine instances):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>reconciles</strong> what the cloud has with what Iki launched: a launch whose machine
        is gone ends, and its worker's jobs are interrupted and retried;
      </li>
      <li><strong>stops</strong> idle persistent machines it doesn't need;</li>
      <li>
        <strong>starts</strong> a machine for each queued job no free machine can take, on the first
        of its environment's templates (by priority, then size) that it fits, up to the template's{" "}
        <code>max_concurrent</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each machine is a <code>Launch</code> with a token for it alone; its worker registers with it
      and may run only what the launch says (<Path p={P} sym="def tick(" />, <Path
        p={P}
        sym="class KubernetesFleet"
      />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/launches.py" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      <Path p="deploy/dev/compute.yaml" /> is a deployment's compute in small. Its{" "}
      <code>backend-small</code> template (<code>backend_pool</code>) is sized as the chart's backend
      worker pod, and most first-party environments are bound to it. Heavier tools get container-tier
      templates of their own (<code>k8s_job</code>, a pod per run), such as
      <code>container-numbering</code> for ANARCI. Each carries a made-up price, so runs there have
      costs to show. The fixture plugins add their own in <Path p="deploy/dev/fixtures/compute.yaml" />:{" "}
      <code>container-small</code> starts a pod per run, at most two at once, and{" "}
      <code>container-warm</code> (<code>k8s_deployment</code>) keeps one pod warm for actions that
      ask for a persistent machine, stopping it after ten idle minutes (<Path
        p="deploy/dev/fixtures/compute.yaml"
        sym="limits: { max_concurrent: 1, min_warm: 1, idle_shutdown: 10m }"
      />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>iki deploy build</code> checks a deployment's compute against the plugins its lockfile
      pins: every binding's templates are big enough for its environment, every action fits at
      least one, and every volume has a way to reach the machines (<Path p={C} sym="def load_compute(" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Which template a run gets" open>
        <p>
          An <code>EnvironmentBinding</code> lists templates with priorities. A run's job goes to the
          first by priority, then size, that is on its tier, fits what it asks for and serves the
          lifecycle its action asks for. An environment with no template on its tier makes the
          action unavailable, and the launch says why (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/runs/catalog.py"
            sym="def availability("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Warm pods">
        <p>
          <code>k8s_deployment</code> templates are persistent: their pods run one job after another
          and start each clean. Each keeps <code>min_warm</code> pods per environment bound to it, and
          stops one idle for <code>idle_shutdown</code> (ten minutes unless it says) when there are
          more. A pod started before its template changed is replaced once idle (<Adr n="0062" />,{" "}
          <Path p={P} />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Slurm is a provisioner of the container tier">
        <p>
          A cluster's worker leases each container-tier job whose first fitting template is one of
          its cluster's, while that template has room under <code>max_concurrent</code>, and holds
          many at once. The server places each job (template, the project's Slurm account, what to
          ask Slurm for); the worker submits it, reports what Slurm says, and masks the log on the
          compute node (<Adr n={[133, 156, 167]} />, <Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/slurm.rs" />,{" "}
          <Path p="docs/slurm.md" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="VM-tier machines">
        <p>
          A vm-tier run gets a cloud machine of its own. Its boot script starts <code>iki-worker
            host</code
          >, which pulls the environment's image, runs it with the same binary as its entrypoint,
          and shuts the machine down when it ends. A spot machine's two-minute warning stops the
          container so the worker checkpoints and reports the job interrupted (<Adr n={[74, 69]} />,{" "}
          <Path p="worker/crates/iki-worker/src/host.rs" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Volumes and setup builds">
        <p>
          An environment can declare volumes its setup script fills, such as a reference database. A
          build belongs to the deployment, not to a project; each provisioner supports some volume
          strategies (snapshot clones, exclusive disks, shared filesystems, bucket mounts). Which
          build a run reads is pinned at launch (<Adr n={[75, 76, 77, 78]} />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/runs/setups.py"
          />, <PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Licensed images">
        <p>
          Some environments run only from an image the deployment supplies, bound in{" "}
          <code>compute.yaml</code>. That's on <PageLink to="Licensed images" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={C} /></li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/catalog.py" /></li>
          <li><Path p={P} /></li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/kubernetes.py" /></li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/slurm.py" /></li>
          <li><Path p="deploy/dev/compute.yaml" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0009" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0041" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0061" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0074" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0112" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0133" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Where the ADRs and code differ</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0009" />'s "client" tier is <code>self_hosted</code> in the code, belonging to teams (<Adr n="0112" />).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
